{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Deprecate REBAR_LLM_MODEL, add ADR and example configs\n\n## Context / Problem\nOnce classes are the interface, `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is a second, ambiguous way to say the same thing —\na single scalar that cannot express a per-tier mapping. Keeping both indefinitely reintroduces the\nambiguity this epic removes. It is also load-bearing today: the production cutover's kill switch\n[rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a] is documented as \"point `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` back at `anthropic:...`\".\n\n## Approach\nDeprecate with a MIGRATION WINDOW, registered via `_scheduled(...)` with the default `remove_in=_MAJOR` (v1.0.0). THIS IS THE PARENT'S DECISION, not a default I chose: eb58 states in bold that \"`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is DEPRECATED with a migration window rather than kept as a permanent alias\", with the rationale that there are few clients today and it is a single-scalar knob. An earlier draft switched to `_permanent(...)` because all six existing env deprecations use it — a real observation, but the WRONG authority AND a miscategorisation. The registry itself says those six are kept forever because they are RENAMES of established names (`_deprecations.py:71-73`); `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is a SUPERSESSION by a different interface, which is what `_scheduled` is for. The parent CONSIDERED the permanent alias and rejected it deliberately, so the six precedents are the repo's general habit and this is a considered exception. Approach and Scope now agree on the window. Within the window `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is still\nhonoured, emits a deprecation warning through `warn_deprecated` (per call — see AC2, the once-per-process form was withdrawn as unmechanised), and maps to ALL THREE classes — the\nwidest-compatibility reading, so an operator who set one model keeps getting exactly that model\neverywhere and nothing breaks mid-window.\n\nDeprecation (rather than keeping it as a permanent shorthand) is a deliberate operator-approved call:\nthere are few clients today, and it yields a single unambiguous config model.\n\nBecause the kill switch's current wording goes stale the moment this lands, rewriting it in class\nterms is part of THIS ticket's contract — a kill switch that is wrong during an incident is worse than\nnone.\n\nShips example configs per provider in docs, so the easy path is documentation rather than presets\nshipped in code that clients select by name.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — **HOSTS A `warn_deprecated` CALL AT ITS OWN ENV READ (`:469`), plus the\n  docstring corrections at `:10` and `:195`.** Two earlier drafts both got this wrong and the reasoning\n  matters, so it is recorded: draft 1 put the call here and ONLY here, which misses the fan-out path;\n  draft 2 over-corrected to model_classes.py ONLY, claiming \"one call site at the read covers every\n  reachable path\". THAT CLAIM IS FALSE. VERIFIED: `config.py:469` performs its own read —\n  `_llm_str(table, cli, \"REBAR_LLM_MODEL\", \"model\", DEFAULT_MODEL)` inside `from_env()` — so a\n  model_classes-only warning is silent on every path that builds a config without touching the\n  fan-out.\n  AND THE WARNING CANNOT LIVE IN `resolve_model_string` AT ALL, which is the deeper reason: `:205-207`\n  calls `resolve_model_string(step or workflow or cfg.model)`, passing an ALREADY-RESOLVED STRING. By\n  that point the value is indistinguishable from a config-table `model` key or a per-step `model:` —\n  so warning there would fire FALSELY for operators who never set the env var at all. Provenance\n  exists ONLY at the env read. Therefore: **the warning goes at EVERY env read of `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`\n  and nowhere else** — `config.py:469` today, plus the new read in `model_classes.py` that the fan-out\n  needs. Two call sites is CORRECT and costs nothing, because emission is per-call by design (AC2) and\n  needs no dedup.\n  `config.py` must still NOT host the class fan-out:\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — **the map-to-all-three fan-out belongs HERE, and it READS\n  `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` ITSELF** (a literal `os.environ.get(\"REBAR_LLM_MODEL\")`, exactly as `:118-126`\n  already reads the nine per-class vars — which is also what keeps it visible to\n  `scripts/gen_env_registry.py`, whose AST scan only records STRING-LITERAL env names). Reading it\n  here is what gives this site the provenance needed to warn correctly, and it carries the second\n  `warn_deprecated` call, beside the slot\n  resolution it has to compose with: `load_class_slots` (`:207`), `parse_class_slots` (`:166`) and\n  `_DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS` (`:47`). A bare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` becomes the default for all three slots\n  at the point slots are built, which is also where the documented per-field precedence\n  (CLI > env > config table > built-in default) is already applied — putting it in `config.py`\n  behind a lazy import would duplicate that precedence logic on the far side of the seam.\n- `src/rebar/_deprecations.py` — **REQUIRED, and previously missing from this Scope.** The\n  deprecation must be REGISTERED here via `_scheduled(...)` in `REGISTRY` (`:62`, `:100`) and emitted\n  through `warn_deprecated` (`:299`), per the parent's migration-window decision. **AND THE BLOCK COMMENT AT `:75-79` MUST BE CORRECTED IN THE SAME CHANGE** — previously\n  unscoped, and adding the entry makes it FALSE. It currently reads: \"This registry now holds ONLY\n  these permanent renames: every remaining SCHEDULED (removable) surface has been removed in the\n  pre-1.0 breaking passes.\" This ticket adds the FIRST scheduled entry since that drain, so the\n  comment must say so instead of asserting the registry is permanent-only.\n  **WHY `_scheduled` IS RIGHT HERE AND THE SIX `_permanent` PRECEDENTS DO NOT APPLY — this closes the\n  question, which has been flipped twice.** The registry documents its own reason for those six at\n  `:71-73`: they are \"stable REBAR_-prefixed renames of established names\" that \"warned 'deprecated'\n  historically, which was a contradiction — they are kept forever.\" That is a RENAME: the same knob\n  under a better name, so it must never be removed. `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is NOT a rename — it is a knob\n  SUPERSEDED by a different interface (per-class slots), which is precisely what `_scheduled` exists\n  for and what the two pre-1.0 breaking passes recorded at `:87-99` actually used and then drained.\n  So the six precedents are precedent for RENAMES, not for supersessions, and an earlier draft of this\n  plan that switched to `_permanent` on the strength of their COUNT was wrong twice over: it\n  contradicted the parent's explicit decision AND miscategorised the change. State this reasoning in\n  the corrected comment so the next reader does not re-litigate it. This is ENFORCED, not stylistic:\n  `tests/unit/test_deprecation_registry.py:125::test_no_raw_deprecation_emissions` source-scans all\n  of `src/rebar/` and fails any deprecation signal that does not route through `warn_deprecated`.\n  A hand-rolled `warnings.warn` in `config.py` would fail that test AND be invisible to\n  `scripts/gen_env_registry.py`, which imports `rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` and unions it into the\n  generated env docs.\n- **USER-FACING STRINGS presenting `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as the CURRENT interface — previously missing from\n  this Scope, and NOT mere comments.** I earlier cleared these as \"comments/docstrings, inert\"; that\n  was WRONG for the first two, which render to operators:\n  * `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py:160` — the `argparse` `description=` for `rebar review`, i.e.\n    `--help` TEXT: \"Needs the 'agents' extra + a model API key (provider per REBAR_LLM_MODEL)\".\n  * `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:64` — an MCP TOOL DESCRIPTION surfaced to MCP clients.\n  * `src/rebar/llm/config.py:10` — the module docstring's env table, presenting it as \"model id\n    (default ``claude-opus-4-8``)\", i.e. THE model knob.\n  * `src/rebar/llm/config.py:195` — `resolve_model`'s docstring, describing it as current precedence.\n  `llm/completion.py:72` and `llm/gate_context.py:14` are genuinely internal comments and need no edit.\n- `docs/adr/` — an ADR recording the class model and the deprecation window.\n- `docs/` — example configs (Anthropic-only, Bedrock-only, mixed-provider, local+fallback).\n- ~~The cutover runbook's kill-switch entry — restated in class terms.~~ **REMOVED FROM SCOPE, and\n  this resolves a contradiction in an earlier draft.** The S8 section below states that this ticket\n  does NOT redesign S8's recovery path, and `infra/runbooks/` is deliberately absent from\n  `file_impact` — yet Scope listed the runbook restatement as a deliverable here. It belongs to S8\n  (`eb6e`), which owns `infra/runbooks/`. This ticket's only obligation toward it is the durability\n  note already recorded on `eb6e` for the operator to resolve there.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tier for the mapping and the warning; docs assertions for the rest.\n- `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set and no classes configured: all three classes resolve to that model.\n- BOTH env-read paths warn, tested SEPARATELY — this is the round-6 defect and needs a test each:\n  (a) `config.from_env()` with `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set and the fan-out never invoked -> warns (the\n      `config.py:469` read);\n  (b) `load_class_slots()` / `resolve_model_string()` reached WITHOUT `config.from_env()` -> warns (the\n      `model_classes.py` read). A model_classes-only or config-only implementation passes one and fails\n      the other, which is exactly the gap two earlier drafts shipped.\n- CLI PRECEDENCE, pinned because two semantics are possible and the suite cannot tell them apart\n  otherwise: `_llm_str` resolves CLI > env > file > default and RETURNS EARLY on a CLI hit, so\n  `os.environ.get` is never reached when `--model` wins. THE CHOSEN SEMANTIC IS WARN-WHEN-SET —\n  check `os.environ.get(\"REBAR_LLM_MODEL\") is not None` at the `:469` call site. An operator who has\n  exported a deprecated variable should be told even when a CLI flag overrides it this run, because the\n  export is still a migration item. Warn-when-env-WINS is rejected: it cannot live inside `_llm_str`,\n  which serves ~a dozen variables and must not learn about one of them, and implementing it at the call\n  site would duplicate the precedence logic this plan already refuses to duplicate for the fan-out.\n  TEST: `--model` set AND `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set -> the CLI value wins AND the warning still fires.\n- NEGATIVE CONTROL, and the reason the warning cannot live in `resolve_model_string`: a config-table\n  `model` key (or a per-step `model:`) with `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` UNSET must emit NO warning. A warning\n  placed on the resolved string would fire here falsely.\n- the deprecation warning is emitted whenever `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is consulted, PER CALL. Not\n  once-per-process: that requirement was withdrawn (see AC2) because `warn_deprecated` has no dedup\n  and none of the six existing env deprecations has any either.\n- classes configured AND `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set: classes win, warning still emitted.\n- with neither set, resolution is unchanged from today's defaults.\n\n## Interaction with S8's kill switch (eb6e) — NOT a safety conflict, but it must be handled\n\nS8 (`eb6e`) documents its production recovery path as \"a documented one-line kill switch\n(`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` back to `anthropic:…`)\", scopes `infra/compose/` and `infra/runbooks/` around it,\nand has an AC to EXECUTE it end-to-end. S8 is blocked behind `eb58`, which cannot close until THIS\nticket does — so the deprecation lands FIRST and S8 would then build a recovery path on a deprecated\nvariable.\n\n**That does not break the kill switch, and this ticket must not be read as breaking it.** VERIFIED: `_scheduled(...)` sets `permanent=False` with `remove_in=_MAJOR` (v1.0.0), so within the window the variable WARNS and keeps working — S8's kill switch is unaffected BY THIS TICKET. But be exact about the limit, because an earlier draft of this plan overclaimed here: under a migration window `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` IS scheduled for removal, so any S8 runbook naming it as the recovery lever WILL rot at v1.0.0. That durability concern is REAL, is recorded on eb6e for the operator to resolve there, and was briefly withdrawn by a draft that had switched to `_permanent` — reverting to the parent's window reinstates it. This ticket does NOT redesign S8's recovery path.\n\nThe paragraph above is the CURRENT and FINAL position on durability. An earlier UPDATE block here claimed the concern \"dissolves under the permanent-alias decision\" — written while this plan briefly used `_permanent`, and now WRONG, because the parent's window means the variable IS scheduled for removal. So the resolution is documentation, not mechanism — no change to S8's safety behaviour:\n\n- this ticket's docs criterion covers recovery documentation too, so nothing it writes presents the\n  deprecated variable as the way to recover;\n- `eb6e` should express its kill switch in CLASS terms (the class slots, or\n  `REBAR_LLM_<CLASS>_MODEL`), which is a strictly equivalent one-line operator action. Recorded as a\n  comment on `eb6e` rather than edited into it, since redesigning another ticket's production\n  recovery path is the operator's call and not a side effect of a deprecation ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is registered in `_deprecations.REGISTRY` via `_scheduled(...)` (NOT\n      `_permanent(...)`), and a test asserts the entry exists with `permanent=False` and a non-None\n      `remove_in`. Previously no criterion covered the REGISTRATION itself — only that a warning is\n      emitted — so the required form could have been silently wrong.\n- [ ] the block comment at `_deprecations.py:75-79` no longer asserts the registry holds ONLY\n      permanent renames, and states why this entry is scheduled (a SUPERSESSION, not a rename). A\n      test or grep assertion pins this: Scope mandates it in bold, but with no criterion an executor\n      could close every AC and ship a comment the code contradicts.\n\n- [ ] the four RENDERED references named in Scope no longer present `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as the current\n      model-selection interface: `_cli/_llm_commands.py:160` (`rebar review --help`),\n      `_mcp_llm.py:64` (MCP tool description), and `llm/config.py:10` and `:195`. These are\n      OPERATOR-VISIBLE, not comments — the first two render in `--help` output and in MCP clients — so\n      a grep assertion pins them and a reader of `--help` is not sent to a deprecated knob.\n\n- [ ] the four example configs named in Scope exist under `docs/` — Anthropic-only, Bedrock-only,\n      mixed-provider, and local+fallback — and each is a COMPLETE `[tool.rebar.llm.model_classes]`\n      table a reader can paste, not a fragment. Added because Scope listed this deliverable with no\n      criterion covering it, so it could have shipped as prose about configs rather than configs.\n- [ ] a test asserts `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` alone maps to all three classes\n- [ ] TWO SEPARATE tests assert the deprecation warning, one per env-read path — NOT one test. This\n      is stated as two because a single test is exactly the round-6 defect: (a) `config.from_env()`\n      with `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set and the fan-out never invoked warns (the `config.py:469` read), and\n      (b) `load_class_slots()`/`resolve_model_string()` reached WITHOUT `config.from_env()` warns (the\n      `model_classes.py` read). A config-only or model_classes-only implementation passes one and\n      fails the other; an implementer reading a singular criterion would write one test and ship the\n      gap. Both route through `warn_deprecated` so the registry is the single source of the message.\n- [ ] a test asserts the NEGATIVE CONTROL: a config-table `model` key (or per-step `model:`) with\n      `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` UNSET emits NO warning. This is the criterion that FORBIDS placing the warning\n      in `resolve_model_string`, where the value's provenance is already lost.\n- [ ] a test asserts the CLI-PRECEDENCE semantic: with `--model` AND `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` both set, the\n      CLI value wins AND the warning still fires (warn-when-SET, not warn-when-env-wins). Without this\n      the two semantics are indistinguishable to the suite and either could ship.\n- [ ] a test asserts that with NEITHER classes nor `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set, resolution is unchanged from\n      today's defaults and NO warning is emitted.\n- [ ] a test asserts explicit class config takes precedence over `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`\n- [ ] an ADR exists recording the class model, the deprecation window, and the rejected alternatives\n- [ ] every doc that presents `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as the way to choose a model presents the class\n      configuration instead, with the env var marked deprecated. The SIX below are the PROSE (`.md`) files ONLY — the four rendered `--help`/tool/docstring strings\n      named in Scope are a SEPARATE class with their own criterion, because an earlier draft of this\n      criterion counted only `.md` files while reading as if the whole repo were covered.\n      RE-MEASURED with `grep -rn REBAR_LLM_MODEL` over the repo root excluding `.git/`, `.venv/` and\n      `tests/` (tests are covered by AC2, not by this prose criterion) — stated as a command rather\n      than as a claim about coverage, because an earlier\n      version of this criterion said \"MEASURED five files\" from a grep scoped to `docs/` and\n      `infra/` only and therefore MISSED THE PROJECT README. There are SIX prose files:\n      `README.md` (line 239 — the front page, presenting `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as THE documented\n      multi-provider interface, so the most important of the six), `docs/llm-framework.md`,\n      `docs/config.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, `docs/workflow-authoring-v2.md`, and the\n      generated `docs/env-vars.md` (regenerated, never hand-edited). Retargeted from an earlier version of this criterion that named the cutover\n      runbook's kill-switch entry: VERIFIED VACUOUS — `grep -rn REBAR_LLM_MODEL infra/runbooks/`\n      returns NOTHING, so no runbook ever offered it as a recovery lever and that criterion would\n      have passed without any work. The kill-switch entry itself belongs to S8 (eb6e), which owns\n      `infra/runbooks/`"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785590361392936001,"uuid":"1e40500e-ee3b-44ba-be53-827cdf1d4cf9"}